"The engine's pointer-record write paths evolve to nest at least some `index.json`/`*.pointer.json` files under a directory matched by a directory-granularity ignore (`jobs/` or a per-lane `raw/`), and no test asserts that every written pointer-record is actually git-tracked, so the negation silently no-ops and the loss is invisible until a clone reveals it.\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"verdict\": \"Coming problem the author has not yet seen: the durability guarantee is unverified and degrades silently; the failure mode is delayed and only visible after the very restart C9 exists to survive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"kind\": \"anticipate\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"future_state_condition\": \"With multiple projects sharing one `.anchor/` folder and concurrent multi-agent/multi-machine runs, the committed pointer-records and the regenerated structure-only `Anchor.md` become a recurring source of merge churn and conflicts.\",\n      \"enabling_assumption\": \"Concurrent agents commit into the shared `.anchor/projects/<id>/` tree (and re-stamp `Anchor.md`'s 'Last scanned' line + counts) without a merge/append-only or per-run-file strategy, so independently-valid runs collide on the same tracked index files.\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"verdict\": \"Forward risk: the shared committed store optimizes for durability but not for concurrent-writer ergonomics; an append-only/per-run record layout would pre-empt the churn.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nitpicks\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"N1\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The committed, user-facing `.anchor/README` and `.gitignore` comments reference internal build-process vocabulary ('Wave 8', 'C9/C10'). These leak internal sequencing into a shipped artifact and will age poorly for an external reader of a distributed project.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"N2\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Redundant coverage: `projects/*/jobs/` plus `projects/*/**/*.log` doubly ignore logs that live under `jobs/`. Harmless, but the overlap signals the directory-vs-pattern mixing that underlies F1.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"elevations\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E1\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"high\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If the writer enforces a static path-allowlist that provably confines every pointer-record to the four lane roots (never under `jobs/` or any `raw/`), the assertion is redundant — the placement invariant already guarantees what the test would check.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Add a deterministic test to the existing suite that enumerates every written/intended pointer-record and asserts `git check-ignore` does NOT match it. This converts C9 durability from a convention into a machine-checked invariant, turning the F1/F2 silent-loss class into a caught, GREEN-or-RED gate at commit/CI time — cheap, and directly aligned with the project's stated reliance on a deterministic test gate.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Make the durability guarantee enforced rather than conventional.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E2\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"medium\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If the consuming reader resolves indexes by lane-relative co-location (it expects `index.json` to sit beside its lane's content), a separated `index/` subtree breaks lookups and the in-place negation is the lesser evil.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Restructure so all durable pointer-records live under a single allow-listed subtree (e.g. `.anchor/projects/<id>/index/`) physically separated from volatile payloads, and ignore payloads by file-pattern rather than wholesale directory. This removes all reliance on filename-negation crossing directory-ignore boundaries — eliminating the F1 fragility by construction rather than guarding against it.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Prefer structural separation over negation that must thread directory boundaries.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"skips\": [\n 